<article class="env-lemma" id="0C2Y">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C2Y">Lemma <span data-tag="0C2Y">10.43.9</span>.</a> Let $k'/k$ be a separable algebraic field extension. Let $A$ be an algebra over $k'$. Then $A$ is geometrically reduced over $k$ if and only if it is geometrically reduced over $k'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $A$ is geometrically reduced over $k'$. Let $K/k$ be a field extension. Then $K \otimes _ k k'$ is a reduced ring by Lemma <a href="/tag/030U" data-tag="030U">10.43.6</a>. Hence by Lemma <a href="/tag/034N" data-tag="034N">10.43.5</a> we find that $K \otimes _ k A = (K \otimes _ k k') \otimes _{k'} A$ is reduced. 
    <p>
      Assume $A$ is geometrically reduced over $k$. Let $K/k'$ be a field extension. Then 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  K \otimes _{k'} A = (K \otimes _ k A) \otimes _{(k' \otimes _ k k')} k'  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $k' \otimes _ k k' \to k'$ is a localization by Lemma <a href="/tag/0C2X" data-tag="0C2X">10.43.8</a>, we see that $K \otimes _{k'} A$ is a localization of a reduced algebra, hence reduced. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>